Everything that writes analytics goes through one interface, and the environment decides which implementation backs it:

```typescript
export interface IAnalyticsStore {
  clearSeriesBySource(source, cleanUpDimensions?): Promise<number>;
  clearEmptyAnalyticsDimensions(): Promise<number>;
  getMatchingSeries(query): Promise<AnalyticsSeries[]>;
  addSeriesValue(input): Promise<void>;
  addSeriesValues(inputs): Promise<void>;
  getDimensions(): Promise<any>;
  subscribeToSource(source, callback): () => void;
}
```

Note what is *not* there: no arbitrary-SQL escape hatch and no wipe method. The interface is deliberately immutable-ish; `PostgresAnalyticsStore` adds a `raw(sql)` method for development and benchmarking only, and the browser store is reset by deleting the IndexedDB database through the standard browser API.

The shipped implementations at 6.2.2-dev.45:

- **`BrowserAnalyticsStore`** (`@powerhousedao/analytics-engine-browser`) — runs Postgres-in-WASM via PGlite, accepts a `PGlite`/`PGliteWorker` instance or a `databaseName`, and requires `await store.init()`. Init is idempotent and creates the schema itself, so nothing pre-provisions the database. This is what Connect's `createAnalyticsStore({ pgLite })` returns.
- **`KnexAnalyticsStore`** (`@powerhousedao/analytics-engine-knex`) — the shared Knex query implementation the others build on.
- **`PostgresAnalyticsStore`** (`@powerhousedao/analytics-engine-pg`) — `extends KnexAnalyticsStore`, takes a `connectionString` or a `knex` instance, uses `pg`, and assumes an already-initialized Postgres schema.

**Doc drift worth knowing:** the Analytics Engine pages document a `MemoryAnalyticsStore` as the everywhere-compatible default and use it in nearly every example, including the GraphQL integration snippet. No such class exists in the monorepo any more — the browser store absorbed the PGlite-in-memory role. Copying those examples verbatim will not compile.

Provenance: drafts `03-Build/04-WorkWithData/_06-Analytics Engine/typescript/{index,memory,browser,pg,compatibility}.md`; interface and store set verified in `packages/shared/analytics/types.ts` and `packages/analytics-engine/{browser,knex,pg}/src/` at 6.2.2-dev.45, which no longer contain MemoryAnalyticsStore.

Everything that writes analytics goes through one interface, and the environment decides which implementation backs it:

```typescript
export interface IAnalyticsStore {
  clearSeriesBySource(source, cleanUpDimensions?): Promise<number>;
  clearEmptyAnalyticsDimensions(): Promise<number>;
  getMatchingSeries(query): Promise<AnalyticsSeries[]>;
  addSeriesValue(input): Promise<void>;
  addSeriesValues(inputs): Promise<void>;
  getDimensions(): Promise<any>;
  subscribeToSource(source, callback): () => void;
}
```

Note what is *not* there: no arbitrary-SQL escape hatch, no wipe method, and no lifecycle. `raw(sql)` and `destroy()` both exist on the concrete classes but neither is declared on the interface, so neither is reachable through an `IAnalyticsStore`-typed reference. `raw(sql)` is on `BrowserAnalyticsStore` *and* `PostgresAnalyticsStore` (the benchmarks and the reactor-api schema bootstrap are its only callers); `KnexAnalyticsStore` has no `raw` at all. `destroy()` just releases the handle — the browser store closes its PGlite instance, the Knex store destroys its pool — so wiping data is on you, and in the browser that means deleting the backing IndexedDB database through the standard browser API (`window.indexedDB.deleteDatabase(name)`, which is exactly what `Persistence.test.ts` does).

The shipped implementations at 6.2.2-dev.45 — three classes, and only two of them are related:

- **`BrowserAnalyticsStore`** (`@powerhousedao/analytics-engine-browser`) — `implements IAnalyticsStore` directly, sharing no code with Knex. Runs Postgres-in-WASM via PGlite and talks to it in hand-written parameterised SQL. `BrowserAnalyticsStoreOptions` **requires** a live `pgLite: PGlite | PGliteWorker` instance — the store does not open the database for you, and there is no `databaseName` option on it (`databaseName` is a prop on reactor-browser's `AnalyticsProvider`, a different layer). `await store.init()` is required; it is idempotent and runs a `create table if not exists` script, so nothing pre-provisions the database. This is what Connect gets from reactor-browser's `createAnalyticsStore({ pgLite })`.
- **`KnexAnalyticsStore`** (`@powerhousedao/analytics-engine-knex`) — the shared base for the SQL-builder path, and the base of exactly one subclass. It is not a base for the browser store.
- **`PostgresAnalyticsStore`** (`@powerhousedao/analytics-engine-pg`) — `extends KnexAnalyticsStore`, takes a `connectionString` or a ready `knex` instance, registers `pg` type parsers, and assumes an already-initialized schema.

So "one per environment" cuts along the browser/Node line, not along the real-Postgres/embedded-Postgres line. `reactor-api`'s server always constructs `PostgresAnalyticsStore` — even in local development, where the `knex` instance it is handed is backed by PGlite rather than a Postgres server, and where the server itself runs `initAnalyticsStoreSql` through `knex.raw` first. Both stores can therefore end up on PGlite; what differs is whether the SQL is built by Knex or written by hand.

**Doc drift worth knowing:** the Analytics Engine pages document a `MemoryAnalyticsStore` as the everywhere-compatible default and use it in nearly every example, including the GraphQL integration snippet. `git grep MemoryAnalyticsStore` over `packages/`, `clis/` and `apps/` returns **zero** hits outside the academy docs themselves — no such class exists in the monorepo any more. `BrowserAnalyticsStore` absorbed the PGlite-in-memory role (the benchmark suite still names its `BrowserAnalyticsStore` variable `memory`). Copying those examples verbatim will not compile. The academy also claims the browser store "sits on top of `MemoryAnalyticsStore` but adds an IndexedDB plugin"; in the shipped code there is no layering and no plugin — persistence is entirely a property of the `PGlite` instance you pass in.

Provenance: drafts `03-Build/04-WorkWithData/_06-Analytics Engine/typescript/{index,memory,browser,pg,compatibility}.md`, which are stale. Interface verified in `packages/shared/analytics/types.ts` (re-exported through `analytics-engine-core`); store set verified in `packages/analytics-engine/{browser,knex,pg}/src/`; environment wiring verified in `apps/connect/src/store/processor-host-module.ts` and `packages/reactor-api/src/server.ts` + `src/utils/db.ts` at 6.2.2-dev.45.

